# Jaguar 1 1/2 Litre four-door saloon, 1935-1949.
#
# The SS Jaguar 1 1/2 Litre, sold after 1945 as the Jaguar 1 1/2 Litre and
# then as the Mark IV 1 1/2. The modelled body is the separate-headlamp
# saloon shown by the 1939 and 1948 photographs, without a wing-mounted spare.
# The earlier 1935-37 body with the exposed spare wheel is not represented.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Jaguar_1%C2%BD_Litre
#
# Dimensions 4.39 x 1.66 x 1.52 m, wheelbase 2.86 m.
#
# Shape reference: no side elevation and no rear view were available. The best
# images are front three-quarters about 40 degrees off and a near-front detail.
# The flank and tail are therefore estimated from those views and the published
# dimensions, and the limitation is recorded.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no period UK document was consulted. Black, Grey, Green
# and Maroon are plain colour names, the common finishes of the period. Weights
# 2, 2, 1, 1, total 6, provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 120 km/h. Comfort 40/390, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 40. Classes 2,3.
# Dates 1935-1949 production, road tail staggered 1955-1958.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/jaguar-1half-litre.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/jaguar-1half-litre.dae. References:
# citycars/references/jaguar-1half-litre/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: no side elevation and no rear view exist; the best
# references are front three-quarters about 40 degrees off and a near-front
# detail. The flank and tail are estimated from those and the published
# dimensions. The early 1935-37 body with the exposed wing-mounted spare is not
# represented; the model is the later body shown by the 1939 and 1948 cars.


obj=citycar
name=Jaguar1HalfLitre(Black)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=2
intro_year=1935
intro_month=9
retire_year=1958
retire_month=6
speed=120
class=2,3
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=40
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=390

